[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_000843","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_000843","source_record_id":"rec_000843","source_corpus":"include","title":"mRNA-LM: full-length integrated SLM for mRNA analysis","abstract":"The success of SARS-CoV-2 (severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2) messenger RNA (mRNA) vaccine has led to increased interest in the design and use of mRNA for vaccines and therapeutics. Still, selecting the most appropriate mRNA sequence for a protein remains a challenge. Se v eral recent studies ha v e sho wn that the specific mRNA sequence can ha v e a significant impact on the translation efficiency, halflife, degradation rates, and other issues that play a major role in determining vaccine efficiency. To enable the selection of the most appropriate sequence, w e de v eloped mRNA-LM, an integrated small language model f or modeling the entire mRNA sequence. mRNA-LM uses the contrastive language-image pretraining integration technology to combine three separate language models for the diff erent mRNA segments. W e trained mRNA-LM on millions of diverse mRNA sequences from se v eral different species. The unsupervised model was able to learn meaningful biology related to e v olution and host-pathogen interactions. Fine-tuning of mRNA-LM allo w ed us to use it in se v eral mRNA property prediction tasks. As we show, using the full-length integrated model led to accurate predictions, improving on prior methods proposed for this task.","doi":"10.1093/nar/gkaf044","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nThe success of SARS-CoV-2 (severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2) messenger RNA (mRNA) vaccine has led to increased interest in the design and use of mRNA for vaccines and therapeutics. Still, selecting the most appropriate mRNA sequence for a protein remains a challenge. Se v eral recent studies ha v e sho wn that the specific mRNA sequence can ha v e a significant impact on the translation efficiency, halflife, degradation rates, and other issues that play a major role in determining vaccine efficiency. To enable the selection of the most appropriate sequence, w e de v eloped mRNA-LM, an integrated small language model f or modeling the entire mRNA sequence. mRNA-LM uses the contrastive language-image pretraining integration technology to combine three separate language models for the diff erent mRNA segments. W e trained mRNA-LM on millions of diverse mRNA sequences from se v eral different species. The unsupervised model was able to learn meaningful biology related to e v olution and host-pathogen interactions. Fine-tuning of mRNA-LM allo w ed us to use it in se v eral mRNA property prediction tasks. As we show, using the full-length integrated model led to accurate predictions, improving on prior methods proposed for this task.\n\n[introduction: Introduction]\nMessenger RNA (mRNA) has become a major focus for the vaccine and pharmaceutical industries [ 1-4 ]. In vaccine de- velopment, mRNA technology enables the rapid design and synthesis of vaccines that instruct cells to produce specific antigens, eliciting a protective immune response without using live\n\nReceived: August 15, 2024. Revised: December 7, 2024. Editorial Decision: January 1, 2025. Accepted: January 20, 2025\n\n© The Author(s) 2025. Published by Oxford University Press on behalf of Nucleic Acids Research.\n\nThis is an Open Access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial License\n\n(https: // creativecommons.org / licenses / by-nc / 4.0 / ), which permits non-commercial re-use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. For commercial re-use, please contact reprints@oup.com for reprints and translation rights for reprints. All other permissions can be obtained through our RightsLink service via the Permissions link on the article page on our site-for further information please contact journals.permissions@oup.com.\n\nattenuated pathogens [ 1 ]. This method proved instrumental during the COVID-19 (coronavirus disease 2019) pandemic, facilitating the fast development of effective vaccines [ 5 ]. Several additional efforts are focused on using mRNA for other vaccines, including Respiratory Syncytial Virus (RSV), flu, and more [ 6-8 ]. [truncated]\n\n[discussion_conclusion: Conclusion]\nmRNA-LM integrates three distinct submodules that collectively provide a comprehensive and accurate SLM for fulllength mRNAs. Such SLMs are critical for developing efficient and safe innovative vaccines based on mRNAs. By building a novel architecture that combines the strengths of three language models, mRNA-LM outperforms existing state-of-theart methods. The integration of the CLIP technique enables the mRNA-LM to develop a more robust and meaningful joint representation across the three distinct mRNA segments. This improved representation not only enhances the model's understanding of the entire mRNA molecule but also leads to better performance in various downstream tasks. Our results draw parallels to the success of CLIP in vision-and-language tasks of [ 37 , 55-58 ], where in our framework the inclusion of modality-specific contrastive losses encourages our model to capture complementary information from each sequence segment, ultimately leading to enhanced predictive performance.\n\nOur initial motivation in using CLIP resulted from several findings regarding the relationships between coding and noncoding mRNA regions. Studies looking at the co-evolution of coding and noncoding regions within the same transcript show that these regions contain both overlapping and distinct information, which collectively influences gene expression levels [ 59 , 60 ]. However, as we show in the Supplementary data, CLIP only marginally improved simpler integration approaches. Several factors may explain this. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Datasets for pretraining UTRBERTs]\nWe used several datasets to construct the input for our models. These included Mammalia reference sequences ( https://www. ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ datasets/ taxonomy/ 40674/ ), human virus with complete nucleotides ( https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ labs/ virus/ vssi/ #/ ), Esc heric hia coli ( https://www.ncbi.nlm. nih.gov/ datasets/ taxonomy/ 562/ ), and yeast ( https:// www. yeastgenome.org/). Both the NCBI and yeast genome websites only provide the full mRNA sequences and the CDSs. To identify the UTR sequences, we aligned the CDS with the full sequence. The section preceding the coding region was designated as the 5 ′ UTR, while the section following it was labeled as the 3 ′ UTR. Following these steps, we ended up with 10 million coding, 6 million 5 ′ UTR, and 4 million 3 ′ UTR sequences. The number of unique sequences for each family can be found in Supplementary Table S1 .\n\n[data_representation: Datasets]\nTo evaluate mRNA-LM, we fine-tune the full model on four labeled human datasets [ 32-34 ]. Dataset [ 32 ] collected and processed transcriptome-wide half-life data for human from 16 publications, resulting in data for over 10 000 human genes from 39 samples. Dataset [ 33 ] contains processed Homo sapiens translation rate data, representing the protein abundance per mRNA per hour in log 10 value, derived from mRNA sequencing and ribosomal profiling experiments. Dataset [ 34 ] provides insights into the relationship between mRNA and protein abundance, covering thousands of transcripts and proteins across 29 human tissues. We focused on the transcript expression in the liver as the target values. In their work, the authors used a classification scheme [ 40 ] to categorize protein expression levels in tissues. This framework divides protein into five classes as follows: 'tissue-enriched' (five-fold above their expression in any other tissue), 'group-enriched' (fivefold above any group of 2-7 tissues), 'enhanced' (five-fold above the average of all other tissues), 'expressed in all' (expressed in all tissues), and 'mixed' (genes that do not belong to any of the previous categories). More information about sequence extraction and data processing is provided in the Supplementary data.\n\nTable 1. Average model performance on the test set across all crossvalidation splits for four downstream tasks [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Materials and methods]\n#### Datasets for pretraining UTRBERTs\n\nWe used several datasets to construct the input for our models. These included Mammalia reference sequences ( https://www. ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ datasets/ taxonomy/ 40674/ ), human virus with complete nucleotides ( https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/ labs/ virus/ vssi/ #/ ), Esc heric hia coli ( https://www.ncbi.nlm. nih.gov/ datasets/ taxonomy/ 562/ ), and yeast ( https:// www. yeastgenome.org/). Both the NCBI and yeast genome websites only provide the full mRNA sequences and the CDSs. To identify the UTR sequences, we aligned the CDS with the full sequence. The section preceding the coding region was designated as the 5 ′ UTR, while the section following it was labeled as the 3 ′ UTR. Following these steps, we ended up with 10 million coding, 6 million 5 ′ UTR, and 4 million 3 ′ UTR sequences. The number of unique sequences for each family can be found in Supplementary Table S1 .\n\n#### Pretraining our models\n\nSee CodonBERT [ 22 ] for detailed information on how we trained the CDS component. In contrast to the coding module, which uses codons as the tokens, both UTRBERTs are nucleotide-based models and treat the nucleotides as tokens directly without any special tokenization. The BERT model we used for these regions follows the model we used for CodonBERT [ 22 ] and has 12 hidden layers, 12 attention heads, a hidden size of 768, and an intermediate size of 3072. The main difference between the two architectures is the maximum sequence length. We set it to 512 for 5UTRBERT since &gt; [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Learning the joint representation of segment sequence using CLIP]\nTo learn the joint representation of the three segment embeddings of 5 ′ UTR, CDS, and 3 ′ UTR obtained from tokenlevel embeddings of the three BERT-based encoders, we use the CLIP approach [ 37 ]. CLIP was originally used to leverage contrastive learning to align image and text embeddings in a shared space. Here, we adapt the CLIP technology to capture relationships between different segments in the mRNA. To use CLIP, we view the three segments' embeddings as three separate modalities of the full-length sequence. We next optimize a loss function that aims to align the embeddings of these different modalities.\n\nSpecifically, we apply a contrastive loss between the 5 ′ UTR and CDS embeddings, and another contrastive loss between the CDS and 3 ′ UTR embeddings. The contrastive loss L CLIP is defined as\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere u and v are normalized embeddings from different modalities (different segment embeddings), sim( · , · ) is the co- sine similarity, τ is a temperature parameter, and N is the batch size.\n\nMinimizing the contrastive loss encourages the model to bring the embeddings of different parts of the same sequence closer together in the latent space, while pushing apart embeddings from different sequences. Specifically, for each example in a batch, the contrastive loss aims to maximize the similarity between the 5 ′ UTR and CDS embeddings, as well as the CDS and 3 ′ UTR embeddings, if they originate from the same mRNA sequence. It also increases the distance between embeddings from different mRNA sequences within the batch. [truncated]","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":4,"line_start":23,"line_end":26,"text":"The success of SARS-CoV-2 (severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2) messenger RNA (mRNA) vaccine has led to increased interest in the design and use of mRNA for vaccines and therapeutics. Still, selecting the most appropriate mRNA seq