ercial re-use, please contact reprints@oup.com for reprints and translation rights for reprints. All other permissions can be obtained through our RightsLink service via the Permissions link on the article page on our site-for further information please contact journals.permissions@oup.com.\n\nattenuated pathogens [ 1 ]. This method proved instrumental during the COVID-19 (coronavirus disease 2019) pandemic, facilitating the fast development of effective vaccines [ 5 ]. Several additional efforts are focused on using mRNA for other vaccines, including Respiratory Syncytial Virus (RSV), flu, and more [ 6-8 ]. [truncated]","original_chars":5581},{"section_type":"discussion_conclusion","heading":"Conclusion","heading_level":2,"line_start":182,"line_end":211,"text":"mRNA-LM integrates three distinct submodules that collectively provide a comprehensive and accurate SLM for fulllength mRNAs. Such SLMs are critical for developing efficient and safe innovative vaccines based on mRNAs. By building a novel architecture that combines the strengths of three language models, mRNA-LM outperforms existing state-of-theart methods. The integration of the CLIP technique enables the mRNA-LM to develop a more robust and meaningful joint representation across the three distinct mRNA segments. This improved representation not only enhances the model's understanding of the entire mRNA molecule but also leads to better performance in various downstream tasks. Our results draw parallels to the success of CLIP in vision-and-language tasks of [ 37 , 55-58 ], where in our framework the inclusion of modality-specific contrastive losses encourages our model to capture complementary information from each sequence segment, ultimately leading to enhanced predictive performance.\n\nOur initial motivation in using CLIP resulted from several findings regarding the relationships between coding and noncoding mRNA regions. Studies looking at the co-evolution of coding and noncoding regions within the sam